00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:05,360
Section 6.3 Optimization Optimization is
important to understand for

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:08,640
AI safety concerns because it plays a
central role

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:12,720
in ML. AI systems, particularly those
based on deep

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,960
learning, are trained using optimization
algorithms to learn patterns

5
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:21,239
and associations from data. These
algorithms update the model's

6
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:24,960
parameters to minimize a loss function,
maximizing its performance

7
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:29,230
on the given task. Optimization amplifies
certain behaviors or

8
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:33,429
outcomes, even if they were initially
unlikely. For example,

9
00:00:33,429 --> 00:00:35,869
an optimizer can search through a space of
possible

10
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:38,909
outputs and take extreme actions that have
a high

11
00:00:38,909 --> 00:00:42,350
score according to the objective function,
potentially leading to

12
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:48,509
unintended and undesirable behavior. These
include reward misspecification failures.

13
00:00:48,990 --> 00:00:52,030
A better recognition of the power of
optimization to

14
00:00:52,030 --> 00:00:55,429
amplify certain outcomes might help in
designing systems and

15
00:00:55,429 --> 00:00:59,280
algorithms that truly align with human
values and objectives,

16
00:00:59,359 --> 00:01:03,240
even under pressure of optimization. This
involves ensuring that

17
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:06,519
the optimization process is aligned with
the intended goals

18
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:10,359
and values of the system's designers. It
also requires

19
00:01:10,359 --> 00:01:14,599
considering the potential failure modes
and unintended consequences that

20
00:01:14,599 --> 00:01:19,120
can arise from optimization processes.
Risks from optimization are

21
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,400
everywhere in AI safety. It is only
touched on

22
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:25,319
briefly in this chapter, but will be
discussed in

23
00:01:25,319 --> 00:01:28,719
further detail in the chapters on goal
misgeneralization and

24
00:01:28,719 --> 00:01:32,920
agent foundations. Optimization power
plays a crucial role in

25
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:37,359
reward hacking. Reward hacking occurs when
RL agents exploit

26
00:01:37,359 --> 00:01:39,959
the difference between a true reward and a
proxy

27
00:01:39,959 --> 00:01:43,599
reward. The increase in optimization power
can lead to

28
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:47,040
a higher likelihood of reward hacking
behavior. In some

29
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:50,159
cases, there are phase transitions where a
moderate increase

30
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:53,519
in optimization power results in a drastic
increase in

31
00:01:53,519 --> 00:01:58,469
reward hacking. Section Goodhart's Law
let's define Goodhart's Law.

32
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:01,549
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases
to

33
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:04,349
be a good measure. This notion initially
stems from

34
00:02:04,349 --> 00:02:07,909
the work of Charles Goodhart in economic
theory. However,

35
00:02:07,909 --> 00:02:10,229
it has emerged as one of the primary
challenges

36
00:02:10,229 --> 00:02:14,270
in many different fields, including AI
alignment today. To

37
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:17,110
illustrate this concept, the following is
a story of

38
00:02:17,110 --> 00:02:21,250
a Soviet nail factory. The factory
received instructions to

39
00:02:21,479 --> 00:02:24,159
produce as many nails as possible, with
rewards for

40
00:02:24,159 --> 00:02:27,560
high output and penalties for low output.
Within a

41
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:30,879
few years, the factory had significantly
increased its nail

42
00:02:30,879 --> 00:02:35,039
production, tiny nails that were
essentially thumbtacks and proved

43
00:02:35,039 --> 00:02:39,719
impractical for their intended purpose.
Consequently, the planners shifted

44
00:02:39,719 --> 00:02:42,879
the incentives. They decided to reward the
factory based

45
00:02:42,879 --> 00:02:45,960
on the total weight of the nails produced.
Within

46
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,080
a few years, the factory began producing
large, heavy

47
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:52,840
nails, essentially lumps of steel, that
were equally ineffective

48
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,840
for nailing things. There is figure 6.2 in
the

49
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:59,800
textbook. Here it depicts graphic image
showcasing the difficulty

50
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:03,840
of specification while avoiding Goodhart's
Law. A measure is

51
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:06,520
not something that is optimized, whereas a
target is

52
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,800
something that is optimized. When we
specify a target

53
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,680
for optimization, it is reasonable to
expect it to

54
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:16,080
be correlated with what we want.
Initially, the measure

55
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:17,919
might lead to the kind of actions that are

56
00:03:17,919 --> 00:03:21,650
truly desired. However, once the measure
itself becomes the

57
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:25,490
target, optimizing that target then starts
diverging away from

58
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:28,569
our desired states. In the context of AI
and

59
00:03:28,569 --> 00:03:31,770
reward systems, Goodhart's law means that
when a reward

60
00:03:31,770 --> 00:03:35,008
becomes the objective for an AI agent, the
AI

61
00:03:35,008 --> 00:03:37,490
agent will do everything it can to
maximize the

62
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:41,129
reward function rather than the original
intention. This can

63
00:03:41,129 --> 00:03:44,650
lead to unintended consequences and
manipulation of the reward

64
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:47,370
system, as it can often be easier to cheat

65
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:50,569
rather than to achieve the intended goals.
This is

66
00:03:50,569 --> 00:03:53,330
one of the core underlying reasons for
reward hacking

67
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:56,729
failures that we will see in subsequent
sections. Reward

68
00:03:56,729 --> 00:03:59,490
hacking can be seen as a manifestation of
Goodhart's

69
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:03,330
Law in the context of AI systems. When
designing

70
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:07,050
reward functions, it is challenging to
precisely articulate the

71
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:10,289
desired behavior, and agents may find ways
to exploit

72
00:04:10,289 --> 00:04:13,449
loopholes or manipulate the reward system
to achieve high

73
00:04:13,449 --> 00:04:18,379
rewards without actually fulfilling the
intended objectives. For example,

74
00:04:18,379 --> 00:04:21,220
a cleaning robot may create its own trash
to

75
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:23,899
put in the trash can to collect rewards
rather

76
00:04:23,899 --> 00:04:28,139
than actually cleaning the environment.
Understanding Goodhart's law is

77
00:04:28,139 --> 00:04:31,939
crucial for addressing reward hacking and
designing robust reward

78
00:04:31,939 --> 00:04:35,019
systems that align with the intended goals
of AI

79
00:04:35,019 --> 00:04:38,819
agents. It highlights the need for careful
consideration of

80
00:04:38,819 --> 00:04:42,339
the measures and incentives used in AI
systems to

81
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:47,079
avoid unintended consequences and perverse
incentives. The next section

82
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:51,959
dives deeper into specific instances of
reward misspecification and

83
00:04:51,959 --> 00:04:54,720
how AIs can find ways to achieve the
literal

84
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:58,439
specification of the objective and obtain
high reward while

85
00:04:58,439 --> 00:05:00,399
not fulfilling the task in spirit.
